If invalidProcess = TRUE, we set conveyors off and maybe also set state = IDLE and skip rest. But we need to still update prevStation1Start etc. Actually we can still update them but they won't be used. But it's okay.

Better: We can just check at start: if firstScan and out-of-range, then set conveyors off and set state to IDLE and maybe also set a flag to skip further processing; but we also need to ensure that we don't respond to start condition. So we can set a variable systemEnabled := FALSE; but we can just handle as above.

Simplify: We'll not overcomplicate; we can just ignore processLength validation because spec only cares about first scan; we can treat that we always have a valid processLength (since it's given by caller). But to satisfy spec, we need to implement detection. Let's implement as described.

Now we need to handle the case where we are at IDLE and sensor1 true and station1Start pressed, but we also need to ensure that processSequence array is accessible for index currentStepIndex. We must ensure we don't exceed array bounds. Since processLength is valid (1..8), and currentStepIndex will be incremented only up to processLength, and we assume final entry is 5. So we need to ensure we don't read processSequence[processLength+1] after finishing. In WAITING_FOR_COMPLETION, after we detect completion, we increment step index. If after increment, currentStepIndex > processLength, we should stop? But spec ensures final entry is station5; so after we process the final step (targetStation = 5) we will go to FINISHED and not read beyond. However we need to be careful: In WAITING_FOR_COMPLETION we read targetStation = processSequence[currentStepIndex] after increment. If currentStepIndex = processLength (the last step) and targetStation = 5, we will move to MOVING and then to FINISHED. After finishing, we won't increment further. So no out-of-bounds.

But we also need to handle scenario where after finishing, we may want to reset to IDLE and start again; we will reinitialize currentStepIndex to 1 again. So fine.

Now we need to ensure that we don't erroneously detect arrival at target while still moving (i.e., sensor may be true at both current and target if product is at both? Not physically). But we can also incorporate logic that we only consider arrival if we have been moving (i.e., we have set direction). However we can rely on sensor detection.

Potential issues: If we start moving from station1 to station2, we set conveyorRight = TRUE. At the same cycle, sensor2 may already be true because there is already a product at station2 (maybe previous product). But that scenario shouldn't happen because only one product in line. But we can handle by also checking that we are not already at target (i.e., we have not already been at target). Since we are in MOVING state, we can treat arrival only if we have been moving for at least one cycle (i.e., we have set direction). However we cannot know if we have been moving for at least one cycle. But we can store a flag movingStarted := TRUE after we set direction. Actually we can just treat arrival detection after we have set direction and we are in MOVING state and we have been in MOVING for at least one cycle. But we can approximate by requiring that the target sensor is true and also the previous sensor for target was false in previous cycle. However we don't store previous sensor states. Could store prevTargetSensor. But simpler: In MOVING state, we can check if target sensor is true AND we have been in MOVING for at least one cycle. We can track a flag movedOnce: BOOL. In MOVING state, we can set movedOnce := FALSE initially; then each cycle in MOVING we check if movedOnce = FALSE, then set movedOnce := TRUE and continue; but we need to detect arrival only after movedOnce = TRUE. Actually we need to detect arrival after we have started moving. So we can have a static variable in MOVING: firstCycle := TRUE. At the start of MOVING we set firstCycle := TRUE. Then in each cycle, if firstCycle = TRUE, we set firstCycle := FALSE and continue (no arrival check). Then after that we check arrival. This ensures we don't immediate detect arrival on same cycle as start.

